window.REV_FLIP=window.REV_FLIP||{};window.REV_FLIP["python-automation"]=[{"id":"python-automation-f0001","card":"q-python-automation-001-1-course-scope-and-libraries","sec":"python-automation-s002","q":"In Python automation scripts, when would you choose <code>pathlib</code> over <code>os</code> for file paths?","a":"<p>Use <code>pathlib</code> for new code. It models paths as objects (<code>Path(\"a\") / \"b\"</code>, <code>.suffix</code>, <code>.exists()</code>, <code>.mkdir()</code>, <code>.rename()</code>), which reads better and handles separators across operating systems. <code>os</code> is still needed for things like <code>os.environ</code>, <code>os.getenv</code> and <code>os.scandir</code>, and it is common in older code. The two interoperate: most <code>os</code> functions accept <code>Path</code> objects.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0002","card":"q-python-automation-001-1-course-scope-and-libraries","sec":"python-automation-s002","q":"Why use <code>requests</code> with <code>BeautifulSoup</code> for scraping instead of <code>urllib</code> and string searching?","a":"<p><code>requests</code> gives a simple HTTP API (headers, params, timeouts, status checks). <code>BeautifulSoup</code> parses messy HTML into a tree you can search by tag and class. Together they replace fragile string or regex matching on raw HTML. Both are third-party packages, so they need <code>pip install</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0003","card":"q-python-automation-001-1-course-scope-and-libraries","sec":"python-automation-s002","q":"Which Python library would you use to read secrets from a <code>.env</code> file, and why not hardcode them?","a":"<p><code>python-dotenv</code> (<code>load_dotenv()</code>) loads the file's key/value pairs into environment variables, and the code reads them with <code>os.getenv</code>. Hardcoded secrets end up in Git history, logs and screenshots, and cannot be changed per environment without editing code.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0004","card":"q-python-automation-002-2-reading-and-writing-files","sec":"python-automation-s003","q":"In Python, what is the difference between opening a file with mode <code>\"r\"</code> and mode <code>\"w\"</code>?","a":"<p><code>\"r\"</code> opens an existing file for reading only and raises <code>FileNotFoundError</code> if it is missing. <code>\"w\"</code> opens for writing, creates the file if needed, and truncates it to empty if it already exists. Use <code>\"a\"</code> to append instead of overwriting.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0005","card":"q-python-automation-002-2-reading-and-writing-files","sec":"python-automation-s003","q":"Why is <code>with open(...) as f:</code> preferred over calling <code>open()</code> and <code>close()</code> manually?","a":"<p>The <code>with</code> block closes the file automatically, even if an exception is raised inside it. With manual <code>close()</code>, an error before that line leaves the file open, which can leak file handles and leave buffered writes unflushed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0006","card":"q-python-automation-002-2-reading-and-writing-files","sec":"python-automation-s003","q":"What does this code print when the line is <code>Ann 30 P</code>?","a":"<p>It prints <code>P</code>. <code>str.split()</code> with no argument splits on any run of whitespace, so the result is <code>['Ann', '30', 'P']</code> and index 2 is the last item.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>line = &quot;Ann 30 P&quot;\nline_split = line.split()\nprint(line_split[2])</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"python-automation-f0007","card":"q-python-automation-002-2-reading-and-writing-files","sec":"python-automation-s003","q":"A script writes results with <code>open(\"out.txt\", \"w\")</code> and every run loses the previous run's data. How do you fix it?","a":"<p>Mode <code>\"w\"</code> truncates the file each time it is opened. Open with <code>\"a\"</code> to append, or write to a new file name per run (for example with a timestamp) if each run should be kept separately.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0008","card":"q-python-automation-002-2-reading-and-writing-files","sec":"python-automation-s003","q":"A script uses a relative path such as <code>open(\"inputFile.txt\")</code> and fails with <code>FileNotFoundError</code> when launched from another directory. Why, and what are the fixes?","a":"<p>Relative paths resolve against the current working directory, not the script's location. Either run the script from the folder that holds the file, pass an absolute path, or build the path from the script location with <code>Path(__file__).parent / \"inputFile.txt\"</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0009","card":"q-python-automation-002-2-reading-and-writing-files","sec":"python-automation-s003","q":"How would you route each line of a text file into one of two output files based on a field in the line?","a":"<p>Open the input for reading and both outputs for writing, ideally in one <code>with</code> statement. Loop over the input file object (it reads lazily line by line), <code>split()</code> each line, test the field, and call <code>write</code> on the matching output. Iterating the file object avoids loading a large file into memory.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0010","card":"q-python-automation-003-3-running-cli-commands-with-subprocess","sec":"python-automation-s004","q":"In Python's <code>subprocess</code> module, what does <code>check_call</code> do and how does it report failure?","a":"<p>It runs the command, waits for it to finish, and raises <code>CalledProcessError</code> if the exit code is non-zero. It returns the exit code (0) on success. This makes failures impossible to ignore silently.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0011","card":"q-python-automation-003-3-running-cli-commands-with-subprocess","sec":"python-automation-s004","q":"Why does <code>subprocess.check_call</code> take a list such as <code>[\"python3\", \"example.py\"]</code> instead of one string?","a":"<p>Each list item is one argument passed straight to the program, so spaces and special characters in an argument need no quoting. It also avoids invoking a shell, which prevents shell-injection problems when part of the command comes from user input.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0012","card":"q-python-automation-003-3-running-cli-commands-with-subprocess","sec":"python-automation-s004","q":"How do <code>subprocess.run</code>, <code>check_call</code> and <code>check_output</code> differ?","a":"<p><code>check_call</code> waits and raises on non-zero exit but does not give you the output. <code>check_output</code> returns the command's stdout and raises on non-zero exit. <code>run</code> (Python 3.5+) is the general-purpose API: it returns a <code>CompletedProcess</code> with <code>returncode</code>, <code>stdout</code> and <code>stderr</code>, and raises only if you pass <code>check=True</code>. Use <code>run</code> for new code.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0013","card":"q-python-automation-003-3-running-cli-commands-with-subprocess","sec":"python-automation-s004","q":"Why is <code>subprocess.run(cmd, shell=True)</code> with user-supplied text risky?","a":"<p>With <code>shell=True</code> the string is interpreted by the shell, so input like <code>; rm -rf ~</code> can run extra commands (command injection). Pass a list of arguments with the default <code>shell=False</code>, and validate any user input.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0014","card":"q-python-automation-003-3-running-cli-commands-with-subprocess","sec":"python-automation-s004","q":"How do you capture the text output of a command in <code>subprocess.run</code>?","a":"<p>Pass <code>capture_output=True</code> and <code>text=True</code> (Python 3.7+), then read <code>result.stdout</code>. Without <code>text=True</code> the output is bytes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0015","card":"q-python-automation-004-4-organizing-files-by-type","sec":"python-automation-s005","q":"In Python, how do you get a file's extension in a case-insensitive way with <code>pathlib</code>?","a":"<p><code>Path(name).suffix.lower()</code>. <code>.suffix</code> returns the last extension including the dot (<code>.PDF</code>), and <code>.lower()</code> normalizes it so <code>.PDF</code> and <code>.pdf</code> match the same category. For <code>archive.tar.gz</code>, <code>.suffix</code> is only <code>.gz</code>; use <code>.suffixes</code> for all parts.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0016","card":"q-python-automation-004-4-organizing-files-by-type","sec":"python-automation-s005","q":"When sorting files into folders with <code>pathlib</code>, which method do you call to create a missing destination folder, and what option avoids an error if it exists?","a":"<p><code>Path.mkdir()</code>. Pass <code>exist_ok=True</code> to skip the error when the folder already exists (and <code>parents=True</code> to create missing parent folders), so you do not need a separate <code>is_dir()</code> check.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0017","card":"q-python-automation-004-4-organizing-files-by-type","sec":"python-automation-s005","q":"What is the risk of moving files with <code>Path.rename()</code> when a file with the same name already exists in the destination?","a":"<p>On Unix, <code>rename</code> silently replaces the existing file, so data can be lost. On Windows it raises <code>FileExistsError</code>. Check <code>destination.exists()</code> first or pick a unique name. <code>shutil.move</code> is the alternative when moving across file systems, since <code>rename</code> can fail between drives.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0018","card":"q-python-automation-004-4-organizing-files-by-type","sec":"python-automation-s005","q":"What does <code>os.scandir()</code> return and why can it be better than <code>os.listdir()</code>?","a":"<p>It returns an iterator of <code>DirEntry</code> objects. Each entry carries cached type information, so <code>is_dir()</code> or <code>is_file()</code> usually needs no extra system call. <code>os.listdir()</code> returns only names, so each type check costs another call. Use <code>scandir</code> as a context manager or exhaust it to release the handle.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0019","card":"q-python-automation-004-4-organizing-files-by-type","sec":"python-automation-s005","q":"In a script that loops over a directory and moves files into new subfolders in that same directory, what pitfall can occur and how do you avoid it?","a":"<p>The folder you create may show up in the directory scan or the loop may treat it as an item. Skip directories with <code>is_dir()</code>, or snapshot the listing first with <code>list(os.scandir())</code>. Test the category lookup on a few names and run the script on a copy of the data first, because moves are hard to undo.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0020","card":"q-python-automation-005-5-parsing-data-files-txt-csv-json-xml","sec":"python-automation-s006","q":"What is the difference between <code>json.load</code> and <code>json.loads</code> in Python?","a":"<p><code>json.load(fp)</code> reads from a file object. <code>json.loads(s)</code> parses a JSON string (the <code>s</code> is for string). Using the wrong one raises an error such as <code>AttributeError</code> or <code>TypeError</code>. The inverse functions are <code>json.dump</code> and <code>json.dumps</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0021","card":"q-python-automation-005-5-parsing-data-files-txt-csv-json-xml","sec":"python-automation-s006","q":"Why must you cast values when reading a CSV with <code>csv.reader</code>?","a":"<p>The <code>csv</code> module returns every field as a string. Numeric comparison or arithmetic needs <code>int(...)</code> or <code>float(...)</code>, otherwise <code>\"10\" &gt; \"9\"</code> compares text and gives False. <code>csv.DictReader</code> gives dicts keyed by header but still returns strings.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0022","card":"q-python-automation-005-5-parsing-data-files-txt-csv-json-xml","sec":"python-automation-s006","q":"What does <code>next(csv_reader)</code> do in this snippet?","a":"<p>It consumes the first row (the header) from the reader and stores it in <code>headers</code>. The <code>for</code> loop then only sees data rows. Without it the header row would be processed as data.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import csv\n\nwith open(&quot;groceries.csv&quot;, &quot;r&quot;) as file:\n    csv_reader = csv.reader(file)\n    headers = next(csv_reader)\n    for row in csv_reader:\n        print(row)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"python-automation-f0023","card":"q-python-automation-005-5-parsing-data-files-txt-csv-json-xml","sec":"python-automation-s006","q":"When opening a CSV with the <code>csv</code> module, why should you pass <code>newline=\"\"</code> to <code>open</code>?","a":"<p>The <code>csv</code> module handles line endings itself. Without <code>newline=\"\"</code>, embedded newlines inside quoted fields can be misread, and on Windows writing can produce blank lines between rows. The Python docs recommend it for both reading and writing.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0024","card":"q-python-automation-005-5-parsing-data-files-txt-csv-json-xml","sec":"python-automation-s006","q":"In <code>xml.etree.ElementTree</code>, what is the difference between <code>find</code> and <code>findall</code>, and what type does <code>.text</code> return?","a":"<p><code>find(tag)</code> returns the first matching child element or <code>None</code>. <code>findall(tag)</code> returns a list of all matching children. <code>.text</code> is always a string (or <code>None</code> for an empty element), so cast it, for example <code>float(price)</code>, before comparing numerically. Check for <code>None</code> before calling <code>.text</code> on a <code>find</code> result.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0025","card":"q-python-automation-005-5-parsing-data-files-txt-csv-json-xml","sec":"python-automation-s006","q":"How do you choose between CSV, JSON and XML for data you must parse in a script?","a":"<p>CSV suits flat tables and is the smallest, but has no nesting or types. JSON handles nested objects and lists with basic types and maps directly to dicts and lists, which is why most APIs use it. XML supports attributes, namespaces and schemas, so it appears in older enterprise formats and configuration, but it is more verbose to parse.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0026","card":"q-python-automation-005-5-parsing-data-files-txt-csv-json-xml","sec":"python-automation-s006","q":"Why is <code>data.split(\", \")</code> fragile for parsing a comma-separated text file, and what is a better option?","a":"<p>It breaks if values contain commas, have different spacing, or are quoted. Use the <code>csv</code> module, which handles quoting and delimiters, for real comma-separated data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0027","card":"q-python-automation-005-5-parsing-data-files-txt-csv-json-xml","sec":"python-automation-s006","q":"What does this print, and what happens if the key is missing?","a":"<p>It prints <code>5</code>. A missing key raises <code>KeyError</code>. Use <code>parsed_data.get(\"apples\", 0)</code> for a default when the key is optional.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import json\nparsed_data = json.loads(&#x27;{&quot;apples&quot;: 5}&#x27;)\nprint(parsed_data[&quot;apples&quot;])</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"python-automation-f0028","card":"q-python-automation-006-6-regular-expressions","sec":"python-automation-s007","q":"In Python's <code>re</code> module, why compile a pattern with <code>re.compile</code>, and what do you get back?","a":"<p><code>re.compile(pattern)</code> returns a <code>Pattern</code> object you can reuse with <code>.search()</code>, <code>.match()</code>, <code>.findall()</code> and <code>.sub()</code>. It avoids repeating the pattern string and keeps the pattern named. The module also caches recently used patterns, so the speed gain is small. The main benefit is readability and reuse.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0029","card":"q-python-automation-006-6-regular-expressions","sec":"python-automation-s007","q":"What is the difference between <code>re.search</code>, <code>re.match</code> and <code>re.fullmatch</code>?","a":"<p><code>search</code> finds the first match anywhere in the string. <code>match</code> only matches at the start of the string. <code>fullmatch</code> requires the entire string to match. All return a match object or <code>None</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0030","card":"q-python-automation-006-6-regular-expressions","sec":"python-automation-s007","q":"What is wrong with this code when the text has no phone number?","a":"<p><code>search</code> returns <code>None</code> when nothing matches, so <code>result.group()</code> raises <code>AttributeError: 'NoneType' object has no attribute 'group'</code>. Test <code>if result:</code> first.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import re\nphoneNumRegex = re.compile(r&quot;\\d\\d\\d-\\d\\d\\d-\\d\\d\\d\\d&quot;)\nresult = phoneNumRegex.search(&quot;no number here&quot;)\nprint(result.group())</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"python-automation-f0031","card":"q-python-automation-006-6-regular-expressions","sec":"python-automation-s007","q":"Why write regex patterns as raw strings (<code>r\"\\d+\"</code>) in Python?","a":"<p>In normal strings the backslash is an escape character, so <code>\"\\d\"</code> triggers an invalid-escape warning in recent Python versions and <code>\"\\b\"</code> becomes a backspace. A raw string passes the backslashes unchanged to the regex engine.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0032","card":"q-python-automation-006-6-regular-expressions","sec":"python-automation-s007","q":"How do you rewrite <code>\\d\\d\\d-\\d\\d\\d-\\d\\d\\d\\d</code> using a quantifier, and how do you capture the area code separately?","a":"<p>Use <code>\\d{3}-\\d{3}-\\d{4}</code> for the quantified form. Add a group around the area code, <code>(\\d{3})-\\d{3}-\\d{4}</code>, then call <code>result.group(1)</code> for it. <code>group(0)</code> or <code>group()</code> returns the whole match.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0033","card":"q-python-automation-006-6-regular-expressions","sec":"python-automation-s007","q":"What is the difference between <code>findall</code> and <code>finditer</code> in <code>re</code>?","a":"<p><code>findall</code> returns a list of all matched strings (or tuples of groups if the pattern has groups). <code>finditer</code> returns an iterator of match objects, which gives positions via <code>.start()</code> and <code>.end()</code> and uses less memory on large text.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0034","card":"q-python-automation-006-6-regular-expressions","sec":"python-automation-s007","q":"Why is regex a poor choice for parsing a full HTML page, and what do you use instead?","a":"<p>HTML is nested and irregular, and a regex cannot reliably follow arbitrary nesting. Use an HTML parser such as BeautifulSoup. Regex remains fine for small, well-defined text patterns like phone numbers or email-like strings.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0035","card":"q-python-automation-007-7-input-validation-with-pyinputplus","sec":"python-automation-s008","q":"What problem does PyInputPlus solve compared with a plain <code>input()</code> call?","a":"<p><code>input()</code> returns any string, so you must write your own loop to check and re-prompt. PyInputPlus functions such as <code>inputInt</code> and <code>inputEmail</code> keep re-prompting until the input is valid and return a value already converted to the right type. It is a third-party package (<code>pip install pyinputplus</code>).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0036","card":"q-python-automation-007-7-input-validation-with-pyinputplus","sec":"python-automation-s008","q":"In PyInputPlus, what do the <code>min</code> and <code>max</code> arguments to <code>inputInt</code> do?","a":"<p>They set the lowest and highest accepted numbers. Out-of-range input prints an error and the user is asked again. For example <code>pyip.inputInt(\"Bags:\", min=0)</code> rejects negative numbers.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0037","card":"q-python-automation-007-7-input-validation-with-pyinputplus","sec":"python-automation-s008","q":"In PyInputPlus, what does <code>inputMenu([\"red\", \"blue\", \"green\"], lettered=True, numbered=False)</code> do?","a":"<p>It shows the choices labeled with letters (a, b, c) rather than numbers, and returns the chosen item's text. The user may type the letter or the item itself. Invalid choices are re-prompted.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0038","card":"q-python-automation-007-7-input-validation-with-pyinputplus","sec":"python-automation-s008","q":"Why validate user input at all in an automation script?","a":"<p>Bad values cause wrong results, crashes, or damage: a typo in an amount, a wrong path, or untrusted text passed to a command or query. Validating at the boundary gives immediate feedback and protects data integrity. Validation is the right tool here, not <code>assert</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0039","card":"q-python-automation-007-7-input-validation-with-pyinputplus","sec":"python-automation-s008","q":"How does <code>int(input(...))</code> in a try/except compare with <code>pyip.inputInt</code>?","a":"<p>Both reject non-numbers. The <code>try/except ValueError</code> version needs a hand-written <code>while</code> loop to re-prompt, and range checks need extra code. <code>inputInt</code> provides retry, range limits and optional timeouts in one call, at the cost of a third-party dependency.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0040","card":"q-python-automation-008-8-error-handling-and-assertions","sec":"python-automation-s009","q":"What benefits does error handling bring to an automation script?","a":"<p>Robustness (the script survives unexpected cases like a missing file or network failure), a better user experience (a clear message instead of a traceback), and easier debugging (you log specific details of what failed).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0041","card":"q-python-automation-009-error-categories","sec":"python-automation-s010","q":"In Python, what is the difference between a syntax error, a runtime error and a logical error?","a":"<p>A syntax error breaks the language rules, so the program does not start (for example a missing colon). A runtime error (exception) is raised while the program runs, such as <code>ZeroDivisionError</code>. A logical error runs without any error but gives wrong results, for example <code>range(5)</code> where <code>range(10)</code> was intended. Logical errors are the hardest to find because nothing is reported.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0042","card":"q-python-automation-009-error-categories","sec":"python-automation-s010","q":"Which Python exception does each line raise?","a":"<p><code>print(x)</code> with <code>x</code> never defined raises <code>NameError</code>. <code>\"5\" + 5</code> raises <code>TypeError</code> (wrong type for the operation). <code>int(\"abc\")</code> raises <code>ValueError</code> (right type, wrong value).</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>print(x)\n&quot;5&quot; + 5\nint(&quot;abc&quot;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"python-automation-f0043","card":"q-python-automation-009-error-categories","sec":"python-automation-s010","q":"When does Python raise <code>IndexError</code> and when <code>AttributeError</code>?","a":"<p><code>IndexError</code> when a sequence index is out of range, such as <code>[1, 2, 3][10]</code>. <code>AttributeError</code> when an object has no such attribute or method, such as calling <code>.append()</code> on a string.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0044","card":"q-python-automation-009-error-categories","sec":"python-automation-s010","q":"Why does <code>10 / 0</code> raise an exception while <code>10 / 0.5</code> does not, and what is the exception?","a":"<p>Division by zero is undefined, so Python raises <code>ZeroDivisionError</code>. It applies to <code>/</code>, <code>//</code> and <code>%</code> with an int or float zero divisor. <code>0.5</code> is non-zero, so the result is <code>20.0</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0045","card":"q-python-automation-010-try-except","sec":"python-automation-s011","q":"In Python, why catch <code>ValueError</code> and <code>ZeroDivisionError</code> separately instead of a bare <code>except Exception</code>?","a":"<p>Specific handlers let you respond correctly to each problem (re-ask for a number, or explain division by zero). A broad catch also hides unrelated bugs such as a typo that raises <code>NameError</code>. A bare <code>except:</code> is worse, since it also catches <code>KeyboardInterrupt</code> and <code>SystemExit</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0046","card":"q-python-automation-010-try-except","sec":"python-automation-s011","q":"What does this code print for the inputs <code>5</code>, <code>0</code> and <code>abc</code>?","a":"<p><code>5</code> prints <code>The result is: 2.0</code>. <code>0</code> prints <code>You cannot divide by zero.</code>. <code>abc</code> prints <code>You must enter a valid integer.</code>. When an exception occurs, execution jumps to the first matching <code>except</code>, and the rest of the <code>try</code> body is skipped.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>try:\n    number = int(input(&quot;Enter a number: &quot;))\n    result = 10 / number\n    print(&quot;The result is:&quot;, result)\nexcept ValueError:\n    print(&quot;You must enter a valid integer.&quot;)\nexcept ZeroDivisionError:\n    print(&quot;You cannot divide by zero.&quot;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"python-automation-f0047","card":"q-python-automation-010-try-except","sec":"python-automation-s011","q":"What do the <code>else</code> and <code>finally</code> clauses of a Python <code>try</code> statement do?","a":"<p><code>else</code> runs only if the <code>try</code> body raised no exception. <code>finally</code> always runs, whether or not an exception happened, so it suits cleanup such as closing a file or connection. Exceptions raised in <code>else</code> are not caught by the <code>except</code> clauses of the same <code>try</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0048","card":"q-python-automation-010-try-except","sec":"python-automation-s011","q":"How do you handle two different exception types with the same response in one <code>except</code>?","a":"<p>Give a tuple: <code>except (ValueError, KeyError) as e:</code>. The <code>as e</code> binds the exception object so you can print or log it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0049","card":"q-python-automation-010-try-except","sec":"python-automation-s011","q":"Why is placing <code>except Exception:</code> before more specific handlers a mistake?","a":"<p>Python checks <code>except</code> clauses in order and uses the first that matches. <code>Exception</code> matches almost everything, so later specific handlers never run. Put the most specific types first.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0050","card":"q-python-automation-011-assert-catches-logical-errors","sec":"python-automation-s012","q":"What does <code>assert condition, \"message\"</code> do in Python?","a":"<p>It raises <code>AssertionError</code> with the message when the condition is False, and does nothing when it is True. It documents and checks assumptions while you develop, so a logical error stops the program at the point it appears.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0051","card":"q-python-automation-011-assert-catches-logical-errors","sec":"python-automation-s012","q":"Why does this assertion fail, and what is the fix?","a":"<p><code>reverse()</code> only flips the order, it does not sort, so the first element (1996) is greater than the last (1925). Use <code>years.sort()</code> to sort ascending, after which the first element is 1912 and the assertion passes.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>years = [1925, 1943, 1968, 1937, 1975, 1912, 1989, 1954, 1920, 1996]\nyears.reverse()\nassert years[0] &lt;= years[-1], &quot;First element is greater than last element.&quot;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"python-automation-f0052","card":"q-python-automation-011-assert-catches-logical-errors","sec":"python-automation-s012","q":"Why should you not use <code>assert</code> to validate user input?","a":"<p>Python removes assert statements when it runs with the <code>-O</code> optimization flag, so the check disappears in that mode. Assertions are for programmer assumptions. Input validation must always run, so use explicit checks and raise <code>ValueError</code> or re-prompt.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0053","card":"q-python-automation-011-assert-catches-logical-errors","sec":"python-automation-s012","q":"Which tool do you use for a runtime failure such as a missing file, and which for a wrong assumption in your own logic?","a":"<p>Use <code>try/except</code> for failures that can legitimately happen at runtime (I/O, network, bad input). Use <code>assert</code> to check internal assumptions during development.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0054","card":"q-python-automation-012-9-web-scraping-with-requests-and-beautif","sec":"python-automation-s013","q":"What are common legitimate uses of web scraping?","a":"<p>Price monitoring, collecting headlines or product details, market and academic research, gathering training data for machine learning, and checking page content or layout in automated tests. When an official API exists, prefer it over scraping.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0055","card":"q-python-automation-012-9-web-scraping-with-requests-and-beautif","sec":"python-automation-s013","q":"What should you check before scraping a website, and what good manners should your scraper follow?","a":"<p>Read the site's terms of service and its <code>robots.txt</code>. Add delays between requests, identify your client with a sensible <code>User-Agent</code>, and avoid overloading the server. Scraping personal data can also raise privacy and legal issues.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0056","card":"q-python-automation-012-9-web-scraping-with-requests-and-beautif","sec":"python-automation-s013","q":"Why might a scraper that works today break next month?","a":"<p>Page structure and class names change, so selectors stop matching. Build checks that detect empty results, keep selectors simple, and re-inspect the page when output goes empty.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0057","card":"q-python-automation-013-html-basics","sec":"python-automation-s014","q":"In an HTML document, what is the difference between <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;body&gt;</code>?","a":"<p><code>&lt;head&gt;</code> holds metadata that is not displayed, such as the title, CSS links and keywords. <code>&lt;body&gt;</code> holds the visible content. <code>&lt;html&gt;</code> is the root element containing both.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0058","card":"q-python-automation-013-html-basics","sec":"python-automation-s014","q":"Why does a web scraper or Selenium tester need to know basic HTML?","a":"<p>Scraping and UI automation locate elements by tag, id, class and attributes. Without understanding the tree structure (<code>div</code> inside <code>section</code>, <code>a</code> with a <code>class</code>), you cannot write correct selectors.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0059","card":"q-python-automation-013-html-basics","sec":"python-automation-s014","q":"What does <code>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;</code> do?","a":"<p>It tells the browser to render the page in standards mode for HTML5. It is a declaration, not an element.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0060","card":"q-python-automation-014-inspecting-the-page","sec":"python-automation-s015","q":"How do you find the right selector for the data you want to scrape?","a":"<p>Open the browser's developer tools (Inspect), hover over elements to highlight them, and note the tag and class (or id). Check several items to confirm the pattern is the same for all, then use that tag and class in <code>find_all</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0061","card":"q-python-automation-014-inspecting-the-page","sec":"python-automation-s015","q":"A <code>find_all</code> call on a page returns an empty list. What do you check?","a":"<p>Re-inspect the HTML for the exact tag and class spelling, and print <code>response.status_code</code> and <code>response.text</code> to confirm you received the page you expect. If the data is not in the raw HTML at all, it is probably rendered by JavaScript, which <code>requests</code> does not run.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0062","card":"q-python-automation-014-inspecting-the-page","sec":"python-automation-s015","q":"Why scrape from a repeating container (such as each product card) rather than collect titles and prices in separate lists?","a":"<p>Parallel lists can drift out of alignment if one item lacks a field. Selecting each item container and then searching inside it keeps title, author and price tied to the same record.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0063","card":"q-python-automation-015-request-parse-extract","sec":"python-automation-s016","q":"In <code>requests</code>, what does the <code>headers</code> argument of <code>requests.get</code> do?","a":"<p>It sends extra HTTP request headers. <code>Accept: text/html</code> tells the server the client wants an HTML response. Headers such as <code>User-Agent</code> are also commonly set because some sites reject the default <code>requests</code> client string.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import requests\nresponse = requests.get(url, headers={&quot;Accept&quot;: &quot;text/html&quot;})</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"python-automation-f0064","card":"q-python-automation-015-request-parse-extract","sec":"python-automation-s016","q":"In <code>BeautifulSoup(response.text, \"html.parser\")</code>, what is the second argument?","a":"<p>It selects the parser. <code>\"html.parser\"</code> is Python's built-in parser, with no extra install. <code>lxml</code> is faster and more lenient but needs the <code>lxml</code> package, and <code>html5lib</code> follows browser parsing rules most closely but is slower.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0065","card":"q-python-automation-015-request-parse-extract","sec":"python-automation-s016","q":"What is the difference between <code>.find</code> and <code>.find_all</code> in BeautifulSoup, and what do they return when nothing matches?","a":"<p><code>find</code> returns the first matching element, and <code>None</code> if there is none. <code>find_all</code> returns a list of all matches, which is empty if none match. Calling <code>.text</code> on the <code>None</code> from <code>find</code> raises <code>AttributeError</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0066","card":"q-python-automation-015-request-parse-extract","sec":"python-automation-s016","q":"Why does BeautifulSoup use <code>class_</code> instead of <code>class</code> in <code>find_all(\"a\", class_=\"x\")</code>?","a":"<p><code>class</code> is a reserved keyword in Python, so it cannot be a keyword argument name. The trailing underscore avoids the clash. Alternatively use <code>attrs={\"class\": \"x\"}</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0067","card":"q-python-automation-015-request-parse-extract","sec":"python-automation-s016","q":"What does <code>.prettify()</code> do in BeautifulSoup and when do you use it?","a":"<p>It returns the parsed HTML as an indented string. Use it while exploring a page to see the structure, not in the final scraper.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0068","card":"q-python-automation-015-request-parse-extract","sec":"python-automation-s016","q":"When would you pass <code>response.content</code> instead of <code>response.text</code> to BeautifulSoup?","a":"<p><code>.text</code> is the body decoded to a string using the encoding <code>requests</code> guessed. <code>.content</code> is raw bytes, which lets BeautifulSoup detect the encoding from the document itself. Pass <code>.content</code> when text comes out with wrong characters.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0069","card":"q-python-automation-016-scraping-multiple-pages","sec":"python-automation-s017","q":"How do you scrape several pages when the URL differs only by a page number?","a":"<p>Loop over the page numbers, build each URL (for example an f-string <code>f\"https://example.com/list?page={n}\"</code>), request and parse each page, and collect the results. Add a delay between requests.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0070","card":"q-python-automation-016-scraping-multiple-pages","sec":"python-automation-s017","q":"Why does <code>range(1, 4)</code> scrape only pages 1, 2 and 3?","a":"<p>The end value of <code>range</code> is excluded. To include page 4 use <code>range(1, 5)</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0071","card":"q-python-automation-016-scraping-multiple-pages","sec":"python-automation-s017","q":"Why call <code>time.sleep(1)</code> inside a scraping loop?","a":"<p>To avoid hammering the server. Rapid requests can trigger rate limiting (HTTP 429), blocks or load problems for the site. A fixed or random delay makes the scraper polite.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0072","card":"q-python-automation-016-scraping-multiple-pages","sec":"python-automation-s017","q":"Show two equivalent ways to turn a list of elements into stripped text strings.","a":"<p>With a list comprehension: <code>[el.text.strip() for el in elements]</code>. With <code>map</code> and a lambda: <code>list(map(lambda x: x.text.strip(), elements))</code>. The comprehension is the more idiomatic and readable form. <code>strip()</code> removes the surrounding whitespace and newlines.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0073","card":"q-python-automation-016-scraping-multiple-pages","sec":"python-automation-s017","q":"In Python, is <code>\"page=\" + page_number</code> valid when <code>page_number</code> is an int?","a":"<p>No, it raises <code>TypeError</code> because a str and an int cannot be concatenated. Convert with <code>str(page_number)</code> or use an f-string.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0074","card":"q-python-automation-017-error-handling-and-troubleshooting","sec":"python-automation-s018","q":"What does <code>response.raise_for_status()</code> do in <code>requests</code>?","a":"<p>It raises <code>requests.exceptions.HTTPError</code> when the status code is 4xx or 5xx, and does nothing on success. Without it, a failed request still returns a response object and the script goes on parsing an error page.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0075","card":"q-python-automation-017-error-handling-and-troubleshooting","sec":"python-automation-s018","q":"Which exception class would you catch to handle both network failures and HTTP errors from <code>requests</code> in one handler?","a":"<p><code>requests.exceptions.RequestException</code>, the base class for <code>ConnectionError</code>, <code>Timeout</code>, <code>HTTPError</code> and others.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0076","card":"q-python-automation-017-error-handling-and-troubleshooting","sec":"python-automation-s018","q":"Why should you pass a <code>timeout</code> to <code>requests.get</code>?","a":"<p>By default <code>requests</code> waits indefinitely for a response, so a stalled server can hang the script. Use <code>requests.get(url, timeout=10)</code> and catch <code>requests.exceptions.Timeout</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0077","card":"q-python-automation-017-error-handling-and-troubleshooting","sec":"python-automation-s018","q":"A scraper gets the page but the data you see in the browser is missing from <code>response.text</code>. What is the likely cause and fix?","a":"<p>The data is loaded by JavaScript after the initial HTML. <code>requests</code> fetches only static HTML. Either call the underlying JSON endpoint the page uses (visible in the browser's network tab), or use a browser automation tool such as Selenium.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0078","card":"q-python-automation-017-error-handling-and-troubleshooting","sec":"python-automation-s018","q":"How do you handle HTTP 429 or rate limiting in a scraper?","a":"<p>Slow down by increasing the delay, honor the <code>Retry-After</code> header if present, and back off exponentially on repeated failures. Cache pages you have already fetched.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0079","card":"q-python-automation-018-10-selenium-browser-automation","sec":"python-automation-s019","q":"What is Selenium and what is WebDriver's role?","a":"<p>Selenium is an open-source framework for controlling a real browser from code. WebDriver is the protocol and per-browser driver (ChromeDriver, GeckoDriver) that receives commands such as click, type and navigate, and executes them in the browser. It is used for UI testing, filling forms, and scraping JavaScript-rendered pages.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0080","card":"q-python-automation-018-10-selenium-browser-automation","sec":"python-automation-s019","q":"When do you use Selenium instead of <code>requests</code> with BeautifulSoup?","a":"<p>Use Selenium when content is built by JavaScript or needs user actions (login, clicks, scrolling, forms). Use <code>requests</code> with BeautifulSoup for static HTML, which is much faster and lighter. Selenium launches a full browser.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0081","card":"q-python-automation-018-10-selenium-browser-automation","sec":"python-automation-s019","q":"What problem does <code>webdriver_manager</code> solve in a Selenium script?","a":"<p>It downloads and caches the driver binary that matches the installed browser version, so you avoid \"driver not found\" or version-mismatch errors. Note that Selenium 4.6+ ships Selenium Manager, which finds or downloads drivers automatically, so <code>webdriver_manager</code> is often unnecessary in current versions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0082","card":"q-python-automation-018-10-selenium-browser-automation","sec":"python-automation-s019","q":"Why must you call <code>driver.quit()</code> at the end of a Selenium script, ideally in a <code>try/finally</code>?","a":"<p>It closes the browser and ends the driver process. Without it, browser and driver processes stay running and consume memory. <code>finally</code> ensures cleanup runs even if the script fails midway.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0083","card":"q-python-automation-018-10-selenium-browser-automation","sec":"python-automation-s019","q":"What is the difference between <code>driver.close()</code> and <code>driver.quit()</code>?","a":"<p><code>close()</code> closes only the current window or tab. <code>quit()</code> closes all windows and shuts down the driver session.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0084","card":"q-python-automation-019-filling-a-form","sec":"python-automation-s020","q":"In Selenium 4, how do you find an element by id and type text into it?","a":"<p><code>el = driver.find_element(By.ID, \"input-email\")</code> then <code>el.send_keys(\"john@example.com\")</code>. The older <code>find_element_by_id</code> helpers were deprecated in Selenium 4.0 and removed in 4.3, so use <code>find_element(By.ID, ...)</code> with <code>from selenium.webdriver.common.by import By</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0085","card":"q-python-automation-019-filling-a-form","sec":"python-automation-s020","q":"How do you select a radio button or tick a checkbox with Selenium?","a":"<p>Find the element and call <code>.click()</code>. Use <code>element.is_selected()</code> to check the current state first, so a click does not accidentally untick a checkbox that is already ticked.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0086","card":"q-python-automation-019-filling-a-form","sec":"python-automation-s020","q":"How can you submit a form with Selenium?","a":"<p>Click the submit button (<code>find_element(...).click()</code>), or send the Enter key to a field with <code>send_keys(Keys.ENTER)</code> using <code>selenium.webdriver.common.keys.Keys</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0087","card":"q-python-automation-019-filling-a-form","sec":"python-automation-s020","q":"What does <code>driver.execute_script(\"window.scrollTo(0, window.scrollY + 200)\")</code> do in Selenium and why use it?","a":"<p>It runs JavaScript in the page that scrolls down by 200 pixels. Use it to bring elements into view, or to trigger content that loads on scroll.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0088","card":"q-python-automation-019-filling-a-form","sec":"python-automation-s020","q":"Why is an XPath copied from browser developer tools like <code>//*[@id=\"input-firstname\"]</code> considered more fragile than other locators?","a":"<p>Copied XPaths are often absolute or position-based and break when the layout changes. An id-based locator survives layout changes, so prefer <code>By.ID</code>, a stable <code>By.CSS_SELECTOR</code>, or a short relative XPath on stable attributes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0089","card":"q-python-automation-019-filling-a-form","sec":"python-automation-s020","q":"Why call <code>driver.maximize_window()</code> before interacting with a page in Selenium?","a":"<p>A small window can hide elements or switch to a mobile layout, so a locator might not find the element or a click lands on something else. A maximized window gives a consistent desktop layout.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0090","card":"q-python-automation-019-filling-a-form","sec":"python-automation-s020","q":"Why does <code>webdriver.Chrome(service=ChromeService(ChromeDriverManager().install()))</code> pass a <code>service</code> argument?","a":"<p>In Selenium 4 the driver path is given through a <code>Service</code> object. The old <code>executable_path</code> argument was deprecated in 4.0 and removed in 4.10. <code>ChromeDriverManager().install()</code> returns the path to the matching ChromeDriver, and <code>Service</code> wraps it so the browser can be launched.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0091","card":"q-python-automation-020-drag-and-drop","sec":"python-automation-s021","q":"In Selenium, what is <code>ActionChains</code> and why is <code>.perform()</code> required?","a":"<p><code>ActionChains</code> builds a queue of low-level user actions (mouse moves, drag and drop, key presses, hover). Methods only add steps to the queue. <code>.perform()</code> executes them. Without it, nothing happens.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0092","card":"q-python-automation-020-drag-and-drop","sec":"python-automation-s021","q":"How do you drag one element onto another in Selenium?","a":"<p>Locate both elements, then <code>ActionChains(driver).drag_and_drop(source, destination).perform()</code>. If a page uses HTML5 drag events that this does not trigger, use <code>click_and_hold</code>, <code>move_to_element</code> and <code>release</code> in a chain, or run JavaScript.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0093","card":"q-python-automation-021-reference","sec":"python-automation-s022","q":"List the main locator strategies in Selenium and when you use each.","a":"<p><code>By.ID</code> is the most stable and fastest when ids are unique. <code>By.CSS_SELECTOR</code> is flexible and fast. <code>By.XPATH</code> handles complex relations such as parent or text matching. <code>By.CLASS_NAME</code> finds by a single class. Others include <code>NAME</code>, <code>TAG_NAME</code>, <code>LINK_TEXT</code>. Prefer ID, then CSS, then XPath.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0094","card":"q-python-automation-021-reference","sec":"python-automation-s022","q":"What is the difference between <code>find_element</code> and <code>find_elements</code> in Selenium?","a":"<p><code>find_element</code> returns the first match and raises <code>NoSuchElementException</code> if there is none. <code>find_elements</code> returns a list, which is empty if nothing matches, so it suits existence checks.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0095","card":"q-python-automation-021-reference","sec":"python-automation-s022","q":"In Selenium, what is a <code>StaleElementReferenceException</code> and how do you fix it?","a":"<p>It happens when you hold a reference to an element that the page has since replaced or removed (after a refresh or a DOM re-render). The reference is no longer attached to the page. Find the element again before using it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0096","card":"q-python-automation-021-reference","sec":"python-automation-s022","q":"A Selenium click fails because the element is not interactable or not clickable. What do you check?","a":"<p>Whether the page has finished loading (use an explicit wait for clickability), whether another element such as an overlay or cookie banner covers it, whether it is scrolled out of view, and whether it is inside an iframe that you must switch into.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0097","card":"q-python-automation-021-reference","sec":"python-automation-s022","q":"What causes <code>NoSuchElementException</code> in Selenium?","a":"<p>A wrong locator, an element that has not loaded yet, or an element inside an iframe or a different window. Re-inspect the locator, add an explicit wait, or switch context with <code>driver.switch_to.frame(...)</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0098","card":"q-python-automation-022-11-selenium-waiting-strategies","sec":"python-automation-s023","q":"Why do Selenium scripts need waits on modern websites?","a":"<p>Pages load content asynchronously (Ajax), so WebDriver may look for an element before it exists. This causes exceptions such as <code>NoSuchElementException</code>, and tests become flaky. A wait pauses until the element is ready.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0099","card":"q-python-automation-022-11-selenium-waiting-strategies","sec":"python-automation-s023","q":"Compare implicit waits, explicit waits and <code>time.sleep</code> in Selenium.","a":"<p>An implicit wait (<code>driver.implicitly_wait(10)</code>) is a global setting that makes every <code>find_element</code> poll up to 10 seconds. An explicit wait (<code>WebDriverWait</code> with an expected condition) waits for one specific condition and continues as soon as it is true. <code>sleep</code> always waits the full time. Explicit waits are the recommended choice for dynamic content. <code>sleep</code> is wasteful and unreliable.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0100","card":"q-python-automation-022-11-selenium-waiting-strategies","sec":"python-automation-s023","q":"Why does the Selenium documentation advise against mixing implicit and explicit waits?","a":"<p>The two can combine in unpredictable ways, and total wait times can become longer than expected because each polling attempt may itself wait for the implicit timeout. Pick one approach, preferably explicit waits.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0101","card":"q-python-automation-022-11-selenium-waiting-strategies","sec":"python-automation-s023","q":"What does <code>driver.implicitly_wait(10)</code> actually set?","a":"<p>A session-wide maximum time that <code>find_element</code> and <code>find_elements</code> keep polling before raising <code>NoSuchElementException</code>. It applies to the whole driver session until changed, and it only affects element lookups, not conditions like clickability.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0102","card":"q-python-automation-023-explicit-wait","sec":"python-automation-s024","q":"In Selenium, how do you set up and use an explicit wait for a button that appears after loading?","a":"<p><code>WebDriverWait(driver, 10)</code> sets a 10-second maximum and polls the condition (every 0.5 seconds by default). <code>wait.until(...)</code> returns the element as soon as the condition is true, and raises <code>TimeoutException</code> after 10 seconds. The element is returned so you can click it right away.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from selenium.webdriver.support.ui import WebDriverWait\nfrom selenium.webdriver.support import expected_conditions as EC\nfrom selenium.webdriver.common.by import By\n\nwait = WebDriverWait(driver, 10)\nbutton = wait.until(EC.element_to_be_clickable((By.XPATH, &#x27;//*[@id=&quot;btn-disable&quot;]&#x27;)))\nbutton.click()</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"python-automation-f0103","card":"q-python-automation-023-explicit-wait","sec":"python-automation-s024","q":"Why is the locator in <code>EC.element_to_be_clickable((By.ID, \"x\"))</code> written as a tuple with double parentheses?","a":"<p>The condition takes a single argument, the locator, which is a <code>(strategy, value)</code> tuple. Writing <code>EC.element_to_be_clickable(By.ID, \"x\")</code> passes two arguments and fails.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0104","card":"q-python-automation-023-explicit-wait","sec":"python-automation-s024","q":"When is a plain <code>find_element</code> enough and when do you need an explicit wait?","a":"<p>Use <code>find_element</code> for elements already present when the page loads. Use <code>wait.until</code> for elements that appear, change state or reappear after Ajax loading, an animation or a state change.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0105","card":"q-python-automation-023-explicit-wait","sec":"python-automation-s024","q":"Why can <code>wait.until</code> finish well before its timeout, and why does that matter?","a":"<p>The timeout is only a maximum. Selenium polls and returns as soon as the condition holds, so a 10-second wait that is ready in 1 second costs about 1 second. <code>sleep(10)</code> always costs 10 seconds, which slows test suites.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0106","card":"q-python-automation-024-expected-conditions","sec":"python-automation-s025","q":"In Selenium, what is the difference between <code>presence_of_element_located</code> and <code>visibility_of_element_located</code>?","a":"<p>Presence means the element exists in the DOM, even if hidden. Visibility means it is in the DOM and displayed (non-zero size and not hidden). Use visibility before reading text or interacting. Use presence when you only need the element to exist.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0107","card":"q-python-automation-024-expected-conditions","sec":"python-automation-s025","q":"Which Selenium expected condition do you use before clicking, and why not just visibility?","a":"<p><code>element_to_be_clickable</code>. It checks the element is visible and enabled. A visible but disabled button would pass a visibility check but fail on click.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0108","card":"q-python-automation-024-expected-conditions","sec":"python-automation-s025","q":"How do you wait for a loading spinner to disappear in Selenium?","a":"<p>Use <code>wait.until(EC.invisibility_of_element_located((By.CLASS_NAME, \"spinner\")))</code>, then continue. This also fixes clicks blocked by overlays.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0109","card":"q-python-automation-024-expected-conditions","sec":"python-automation-s025","q":"What does this Selenium code wait for?","a":"<p>It waits until the element with id <code>message</code> contains the text <code>Success</code>, or raises <code>TimeoutException</code>. Useful for validation or status messages that appear after an action.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>wait.until(EC.text_to_be_present_in_element((By.ID, &#x27;message&#x27;), &#x27;Success&#x27;))</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"python-automation-f0110","card":"q-python-automation-025-timeouts-and-multiple-conditions","sec":"python-automation-s026","q":"How do you handle a Selenium wait that times out?","a":"<p>Wrap it in <code>try/except TimeoutException</code> (from <code>selenium.common.exceptions</code>). In the handler, log or take a screenshot, retry, or fail the test with a clear message. A timeout usually means the element never appeared, the locator is wrong, or the timeout is too short.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0111","card":"q-python-automation-025-timeouts-and-multiple-conditions","sec":"python-automation-s026","q":"In Selenium, how do you continue when either of two buttons might appear first?","a":"<p>Use <code>EC.any_of(cond1, cond2)</code>, which returns when at least one condition is met, for example two <code>element_to_be_clickable</code> conditions on different ids. <code>any_of</code> is in Selenium 4 and later. <code>EC.all_of</code> waits for all.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0112","card":"q-python-automation-025-timeouts-and-multiple-conditions","sec":"python-automation-s026","q":"A Selenium wait keeps raising <code>TimeoutException</code> even though the element is visible in the browser. What do you check?","a":"<p>Check the locator (strategy and value), whether the element sits in an iframe, and whether the condition matches the state (for example clickable versus visible). Also confirm the element is not covered by an overlay. Raise the timeout only after ruling these out.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0113","card":"q-python-automation-026-12-working-with-apis","sec":"python-automation-s027","q":"What is an API and why do automation scripts use one instead of scraping?","a":"<p>An API is a defined interface through which programs exchange data. It returns structured data (usually JSON), is more stable than page markup, and is usually allowed by the provider. Scraping HTML is a fallback when no API exists.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0114","card":"q-python-automation-026-12-working-with-apis","sec":"python-automation-s027","q":"Why read the API documentation before writing a client?","a":"<p>The docs define the base URL, endpoints, parameter names, authentication, response format, rate limits and error codes. Guessing leads to 400 or 401 errors.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0115","card":"q-python-automation-027-get-with-parameters","sec":"python-automation-s028","q":"In <code>requests.get(url, params={...})</code>, what does <code>params</code> do and why is it better than building the query string by hand?","a":"<p>It encodes the dictionary into the URL query string (<code>?upc=025000044908</code>) and handles URL-escaping of special characters. <code>response.url</code> shows the final URL. Manual concatenation is error-prone for spaces and symbols.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0116","card":"q-python-automation-027-get-with-parameters","sec":"python-automation-s028","q":"What are the differences between <code>response.text</code>, <code>response.content</code> and <code>response.json()</code> in <code>requests</code>?","a":"<p><code>.text</code> is the body decoded as a string, <code>.content</code> is raw bytes (for images or files), and <code>.json()</code> parses the body as JSON into Python objects. <code>.json()</code> raises an error if the body is not valid JSON.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0117","card":"q-python-automation-027-get-with-parameters","sec":"python-automation-s028","q":"What does the following print?","a":"<p><code>https://example.com/search?q=a+b</code>. <code>requests</code> URL-encodes the space as <code>+</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import requests\nresponse = requests.get(&quot;https://example.com/search&quot;, params={&quot;q&quot;: &quot;a b&quot;})\nprint(response.url)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"python-automation-f0118","card":"q-python-automation-028-parsing-a-json-response","sec":"python-automation-s029","q":"How do you read the title of the first item from <code>{\"items\": [{\"title\": \"X\"}]}</code> that has been parsed into <code>info</code>?","a":"<p><code>info[\"items\"][0][\"title\"]</code>. The top level is a dict, <code>items</code> is a list, and the first element is a dict. Each <code>[...]</code> goes one level deeper.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0119","card":"q-python-automation-028-parsing-a-json-response","sec":"python-automation-s029","q":"What is the difference between <code>json.loads(response.text)</code> and <code>response.json()</code>?","a":"<p>They produce the same result. <code>response.json()</code> is a shortcut that decodes and parses in one step. Both raise a decode error for invalid JSON (<code>json.JSONDecodeError</code>, or <code>requests.exceptions.JSONDecodeError</code> in requests 2.27+).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0120","card":"q-python-automation-028-parsing-a-json-response","sec":"python-automation-s029","q":"How do you avoid a <code>KeyError</code> when an optional JSON field might be missing?","a":"<p>Use <code>dict.get(\"price\", \"N/A\")</code> to supply a default, and check list length before indexing <code>[0]</code> to avoid <code>IndexError</code> when <code>items</code> is empty.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0121","card":"q-python-automation-028-parsing-a-json-response","sec":"python-automation-s029","q":"What exceptions can occur when you parse a nested API response, and where do they come from?","a":"<p><code>json.JSONDecodeError</code> when the body is not JSON (for example an HTML error page), <code>KeyError</code> when an expected key is missing, and <code>IndexError</code> or <code>TypeError</code> when a list is empty or the structure differs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0122","card":"q-python-automation-029-http-methods-and-status-codes","sec":"python-automation-s030","q":"What are the typical HTTP methods in a REST API and what does each do?","a":"<p>GET retrieves data, POST creates, PUT replaces or updates a resource, DELETE removes it. GET, PUT and DELETE are idempotent (repeating them gives the same state), POST is not. PATCH is used for partial updates.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0123","card":"q-python-automation-029-http-methods-and-status-codes","sec":"python-automation-s030","q":"In <code>requests</code>, what is the difference between <code>requests.post(url, json=data)</code> and <code>requests.post(url, data=data)</code>?","a":"<p><code>json=</code> serializes the dict to a JSON body and sets <code>Content-Type: application/json</code>, so you do not call <code>json.dumps</code> yourself. <code>data=</code> sends form-encoded fields (<code>application/x-www-form-urlencoded</code>) for a dict. Use the one the API expects.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0124","card":"q-python-automation-029-http-methods-and-status-codes","sec":"python-automation-s030","q":"What do status codes 200, 400, 401, 404 and 500 mean and what do you do about each?","a":"<p>200: success, process the data. 400: bad request, check your parameters. 401: unauthorized, check the API key or token. 404: not found, check the URL or id. 500: server error, retry later with backoff since it is not your request's fault.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0125","card":"q-python-automation-029-http-methods-and-status-codes","sec":"python-automation-s030","q":"What is the difference between 401 and 403?","a":"<p>401 means the request lacks valid credentials (not authenticated). 403 means the server knows who you are but you are not allowed to access that resource.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0126","card":"q-python-automation-029-http-methods-and-status-codes","sec":"python-automation-s030","q":"Why check <code>response.status_code</code> before parsing the body?","a":"<p>Error responses often have a different body (an HTML page or an error JSON), so parsing as success data raises confusing errors or yields wrong results. Check for 200, or call <code>raise_for_status()</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0127","card":"q-python-automation-029-http-methods-and-status-codes","sec":"python-automation-s030","q":"Which status codes should a client retry and which not?","a":"<p>Retry transient failures: 429, 500, 502, 503, 504, and network timeouts, with exponential backoff. Do not retry most other 4xx errors such as 400, 401, 404, because the same request will fail again.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0128","card":"q-python-automation-030-robust-request-pattern","sec":"python-automation-s031","q":"Why are <code>RequestException</code>, <code>JSONDecodeError</code> and <code>KeyError</code> caught in separate handlers in an API client?","a":"<p>They signal different failures: the request failed (network or HTTP status), the body was not valid JSON, or the data lacked an expected field. Separate handlers give accurate messages and allow different recovery, such as retrying the request but not the parse error.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0129","card":"q-python-automation-030-robust-request-pattern","sec":"python-automation-s031","q":"What does this snippet do and why is <code>raise_for_status()</code> there?","a":"<p>It makes the request, turns any 4xx or 5xx status into an <code>HTTPError</code>, then parses the body. Any <code>RequestException</code> (connection, timeout or HTTP error) is caught and reported. Without <code>raise_for_status()</code>, an error page would be parsed as if it were data.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>try:\n    response = requests.get(base_url, params=parameters)\n    response.raise_for_status()\n    data = json.loads(response.text)\nexcept requests.exceptions.RequestException as e:\n    print(f&quot;Request error: {e}&quot;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"python-automation-f0130","card":"q-python-automation-030-robust-request-pattern","sec":"python-automation-s031","q":"How do you reduce API calls when your script requests the same data repeatedly?","a":"<p>Cache responses (in a dict, file, or with a library such as <code>requests-cache</code>), respect rate limits with delays, and batch requests if the API supports it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0131","card":"q-python-automation-031-13-api-keys","sec":"python-automation-s032","q":"Why do API providers require keys?","a":"<p>For authentication, access control, rate limiting and usage tracking per client, and abuse prevention. A key identifies who is calling.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0132","card":"q-python-automation-031-13-api-keys","sec":"python-automation-s032","q":"How is an API key usually sent with a request?","a":"<p>As a query parameter (for example <code>appid=...</code> in OpenWeatherMap) or in a header such as <code>Authorization: Bearer ...</code> or a custom header like <code>X-API-Key</code>. Follow the provider's documentation. Headers are safer because URLs end up in logs, browser history and proxies.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0133","card":"q-python-automation-031-13-api-keys","sec":"python-automation-s032","q":"A request to an API returns 401 after you added the key. What do you check?","a":"<p>That the key is present and typed correctly, is activated (some providers need a delay after sign-up), is sent in the parameter or header name the docs require, and has not expired or been revoked. Also confirm you are using the key for the right environment.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0134","card":"q-python-automation-031-13-api-keys","sec":"python-automation-s032","q":"Why is sending an API key in the query string over plain <code>http://</code> a security concern?","a":"<p>Over plain HTTP the URL, including the key in the query string, travels unencrypted. Use HTTPS when the provider supports it, so the key cannot be read on the network.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0135","card":"q-python-automation-032-keeping-keys-out-of-code","sec":"python-automation-s033","q":"How does this code load an API key from a <code>.env</code> file in Python?","a":"<p><code>load_dotenv()</code> reads the <code>.env</code> file and sets its entries as environment variables for the process. <code>os.getenv(\"WEATHER_API_KEY\")</code> reads one, returning <code>None</code> if it is not set. Check for <code>None</code> and fail with a clear message.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import os\nfrom dotenv import load_dotenv\n\nload_dotenv()\napi_key = os.getenv(&quot;WEATHER_API_KEY&quot;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"python-automation-f0136","card":"q-python-automation-032-keeping-keys-out-of-code","sec":"python-automation-s033","q":"Why must <code>.env</code> be in <code>.gitignore</code>, and what do you do if a key was already committed?","a":"<p>A committed key stays in Git history and may be exposed publicly or to anyone with repo access. Add <code>.env</code> to <code>.gitignore</code> before the first commit. If a key was committed, revoke and rotate it immediately, because deleting the file in a later commit does not remove it from history.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0137","card":"q-python-automation-032-keeping-keys-out-of-code","sec":"python-automation-s033","q":"What are good practices for managing API keys?","a":"<p>Keep them in environment variables or a secrets manager, never in source code. Never commit them. Rotate keys regularly to limit the damage of a leak. Use separate keys for development and production, and give each key the minimum scope it needs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0138","card":"q-python-automation-032-keeping-keys-out-of-code","sec":"python-automation-s033","q":"When is a <code>.env</code> file not enough for secrets?","a":"<p>In production, where teams use a secrets manager (such as AWS Secrets Manager or HashiCorp Vault) or the platform's injected environment variables. These add access control, audit logs and rotation. A <code>.env</code> file is a local development convenience.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0139","card":"q-python-automation-033-14-api-chaining","sec":"python-automation-s034","q":"What is API chaining and what is a typical example?","a":"<p>Several API calls in sequence, where the output of one call feeds the next. For example a geolocation API returns a city, which goes into a weather API. It builds complex workflows from small services.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0140","card":"q-python-automation-033-14-api-chaining","sec":"python-automation-s034","q":"How do you decide between sequential and parallel API calls?","a":"<p>Use sequential calls when a request depends on data from the previous response. Use parallel calls when the requests are independent, to cut total time. Parallel calls still need rate-limit awareness.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0141","card":"q-python-automation-033-14-api-chaining","sec":"python-automation-s034","q":"What problems can API chaining cause and how do you mitigate them?","a":"<p>One failing call breaks the rest, latency adds up, and an error can propagate silently. Mitigate with error handling and <code>raise_for_status()</code> at each step, timeouts, retries with backoff for transient errors, and logging of each step.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0142","card":"q-python-automation-034-weather-chain","sec":"python-automation-s035","q":"In this two-step chain, why must the second request run after the first?","a":"<p>The weather request needs <code>city</code>, which only exists after the first response is parsed. Order is set by the data dependency.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>location_response = requests.get(&quot;https://ipapi.co/json/&quot;)\ncity = json.loads(location_response.text)[&quot;city&quot;]\nweather_params = {&quot;q&quot;: city, &quot;appid&quot;: &quot;YOUR_API_KEY&quot;}</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"python-automation-f0143","card":"q-python-automation-034-weather-chain","sec":"python-automation-s035","q":"What can go wrong with the key lookup <code>[\"city\"]</code> in a chained call and how do you harden it?","a":"<p>If the first API fails or returns a different shape, <code>[\"city\"]</code> raises <code>KeyError</code>. Check the status code, use <code>.get</code> with a fallback or explicit check, and stop the chain with a clear error rather than sending a bad value into the next call.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0144","card":"q-python-automation-034-weather-chain","sec":"python-automation-s035","q":"Why does the OpenWeatherMap API return a temperature around 290 for a mild day by default?","a":"<p>It returns temperatures in Kelvin unless you set the <code>units</code> parameter. Pass <code>units=metric</code> for Celsius or <code>units=imperial</code> for Fahrenheit.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0145","card":"q-python-automation-035-e-commerce-chain-with-a-conditional-post","sec":"python-automation-s036","q":"In a chain that fetches a product, checks inventory and then charges payment, why check inventory before the POST?","a":"<p>A charge is a side effect that is hard to undo. Verify the preconditions with read-only GET calls first, and only perform the state-changing POST when they pass.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0146","card":"q-python-automation-035-e-commerce-chain-with-a-conditional-post","sec":"python-automation-s036","q":"What is a risk of retrying a payment POST after a timeout and how do payment APIs handle it?","a":"<p>The first request may have succeeded, so a retry can charge twice. Many payment APIs support an idempotency key header, so repeating the same request with the same key is processed only once.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0147","card":"q-python-automation-036-robust-chain-template","sec":"python-automation-s037","q":"Why call <code>raise_for_status()</code> after every step of an API chain?","a":"<p>A failed step must stop the chain. If step 2 returns an error body and the chain keeps going, step 3 would use bad data or fail with a confusing <code>KeyError</code>. Checking each step localizes the failure.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0148","card":"q-python-automation-036-robust-chain-template","sec":"python-automation-s037","q":"Why does a chain function catch <code>RequestException</code> separately from <code>(JSONDecodeError, KeyError)</code>?","a":"<p><code>RequestException</code> covers the transport and HTTP layer (network, timeouts, 4xx/5xx), while <code>JSONDecodeError</code> and <code>KeyError</code> cover data-processing failures. Separating them gives distinct messages and lets you retry only the transport failures.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0149","card":"q-python-automation-036-robust-chain-template","sec":"python-automation-s037","q":"Why might a chain function return <code>None</code> on failure rather than letting the exception propagate, and what is the trade-off?","a":"<p>A <code>None</code> return lets the caller decide what to do without a crash, but callers must check for it, and it can hide the cause. Raising a custom exception or logging the error preserves more detail. The right choice depends on whether callers can recover.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0150","card":"q-python-automation-037-shared-headers-and-versioning","sec":"python-automation-s038","q":"Why define a shared <code>headers</code> dict for several requests in a chain?","a":"<p>It avoids repeating authentication and content-type headers, and one change updates every call. In <code>requests</code>, a <code>Session</code> with <code>session.headers.update(...)</code> does this and also reuses connections.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0151","card":"q-python-automation-037-shared-headers-and-versioning","sec":"python-automation-s038","q":"Why does <code>Content-Type: application/json</code> matter on a POST?","a":"<p>It tells the server how to parse the body. If it is missing or wrong, the server may reject the request (400 or 415) or misread the data. With <code>requests</code>, <code>json=</code> sets it automatically.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0152","card":"q-python-automation-037-shared-headers-and-versioning","sec":"python-automation-s038","q":"Why pin an API version in the URL (such as <code>/v2/</code>) or a header?","a":"<p>Providers change responses over time. A pinned version keeps your parsing stable and prevents sudden breakage when the default version moves. It also makes upgrades a deliberate step.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0153","card":"q-python-automation-038-sequential-vs-parallel","sec":"python-automation-s039","q":"In Python, how do you call several independent URLs in parallel with <code>requests</code>?","a":"<p><code>ThreadPoolExecutor</code> runs <code>fetch_url</code> for each URL in worker threads. <code>executor.map</code> returns results in input order. Threads fit here because the work is I/O-bound, so threads wait on the network while others run. The <code>with</code> block waits for completion and shuts the pool down.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import concurrent.futures\n\nwith concurrent.futures.ThreadPoolExecutor() as executor:\n    responses = list(executor.map(fetch_url, urls))</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"python-automation-f0154","card":"q-python-automation-038-sequential-vs-parallel","sec":"python-automation-s039","q":"Why are threads, not processes, usually used for parallel HTTP calls in Python?","a":"<p>HTTP calls spend their time waiting on I/O, and the GIL is released while waiting, so threads give a real speedup with low overhead. Processes suit CPU-bound work. <code>asyncio</code> with an async client is another option for many concurrent requests.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0155","card":"q-python-automation-038-sequential-vs-parallel","sec":"python-automation-s039","q":"What must you watch for when running API calls in parallel?","a":"<p>Rate limits (many calls at once can trigger 429), error handling per call (one failure should not hide the others), and keeping the worker count bounded with <code>max_workers</code>. <code>executor.map</code> re-raises a worker's exception when you read its result.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0156","card":"q-python-automation-039-rapidapi-marketplace","sec":"python-automation-s040","q":"In RapidAPI, how are the key and host sent with a request?","a":"<p>As HTTP headers: one for your RapidAPI key, one for the host of the API you call. Header names have changed over time, so confirm them in the provider's current docs. Keep the key in an environment variable, not in code.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>headers = {\n    &quot;X-RapidAPI-Key&quot;: &quot;YOUR_RAPIDAPI_KEY&quot;,\n    &quot;X-RapidAPI-Host&quot;: &quot;api-host.rapidapi.com&quot;\n}\nresponse = requests.get(&quot;https://api.rapidapi.com/endpoint&quot;, headers=headers)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"python-automation-f0157","card":"q-python-automation-040-chain-troubleshooting","sec":"python-automation-s041","q":"A multi-step API chain breaks at the second call. How do you troubleshoot it?","a":"<p>Log each step's URL, status code and a trimmed body. Check that the value passed from step 1 is present and correctly formed, then check authentication and rate limits on the second API. Use <code>raise_for_status()</code> at each step so the failing step is clear.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0158","card":"q-python-automation-040-chain-troubleshooting","sec":"python-automation-s041","q":"A chain of API calls is slow. How do you speed it up?","a":"<p>Run independent calls in parallel (threads or async), cache responses you reuse, and reduce calls by batching if the API supports it. Calls that depend on earlier results must stay sequential.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"python-automation-f0159","card":"q-python-automation-040-chain-troubleshooting","sec":"python-automation-s041","q":"A chain that worked last month now returns data in a different shape. What is the likely cause?","a":"<p>The API version changed. Check the provider's changelog, pin the version in the URL or header, and add tests or checks on the response structure.</p>","hand":true}];
